Job


A Kubernetes Job is used to run a task one time until completion.

Unlike Deployments, Jobs are NOT for continuously running applications.


Main Purpose of Job

A Job ensures:

Task runs successfully and completes

even if:

pod crashes

node fails

container exits unexpectedly

Kubernetes automatically retries.

Deployment vs Job

Feature			Deployment				Job
Purpose			Long-running app	One-time task
Pod Behavior	Always running		Runs and exits
Restart			Keeps alive forever	Stops after success
Example			FastAPI app			DB migration


Real Use Cases

1. Database Migration

Example:

Run Alembic migration once

	python manage.py migrate


2. Backup Job

	Take PostgreSQL backup every night

3. Batch Processing

	Process 1 million records

4. ML Training

	Train model once

5. File Conversion

	Convert uploaded videos

6. Data Import

	Import CSV into DB

7. Cleanup

	Delete old logs/files
	Simple Job Example

create 

job.yaml
-------------------
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job

metadata:
  name: hello-job

spec:
  template:

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: hello

          image: busybox

          command:
            - sh
            - -c
            - echo "Hello Kubernetes Job"

      restartPolicy: Never

=====================================
Apply Job

kubectl apply -f job.yaml

Check Jobs
kubectl get jobs


Example:

NAME        COMPLETIONS   DURATION   AGE
hello-job   1/1           5s         10s

=========================================
Check Pods

kubectl get pods

Pod status:

Completed

Check Logs

kubectl logs job/hello-job

Output:

	Hello Kubernetes Job

	Important Field

	restartPolicy

Usually:

	restartPolicy: Never

or:

restartPolicy: OnFailure

	Parallel Jobs

Run multiple tasks simultaneously.

Example:

	parallelism: 3
	completions: 6

Meaning:

	3 pods run at same time

	total 6 successful executions required